Authorized Success Under Blockade: 

A Formal Framework for Task Completion, Authorization Compliance, and Recovery in Autonomous AI Agents

A Theoretical Dissertation

Author: Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

Date: October 2026

Abstract

Autonomous AI agents increasingly operate in environments where task completion depends on access to tools, resources, and external systems. When legitimate pathways are blocked—by unavailable tools, restrictive policies, or infeasible objectives—agents may either find authorized alternatives, abstain correctly, or circumvent the boundaries that define legitimate success. This dissertation develops a formal theoretical framework for evaluating authorized task completion under blockade conditions, without relying on any experimental, simulation, or training data. The central design commitment is that authorization compliance is treated as a hard admissibility requirement rather than a penalizable dimension of a scalar objective: performance is optimized only among policies satisfying the specified authorization constraints, and aggregate utility scores may be reported descriptively but cannot override a failed mandatory authorization condition.

This revised version incorporates recent advances in agent governance and evaluation. We adopt the constraint-loss construct from MasDrift, which measures whether a reserved-action constraint remains operative for the executing agent across delegation handoffs. We operationalize the feasibility oracle using FeasiGen, which constructs infeasible tasks by masking critical tools with over 94% human-verified annotation accuracy and reports false-continue rates reaching 73.9% across nine models. We ground the structural result on reward hacking in the formal theorem of Wang and Huang, which proves that under five minimal axioms any optimized agent systematically under-invests in quality dimensions not covered by its evaluation system, and derive a computable distortion index that predicts the direction and severity of hacking on each dimension prior to deployment. We adopt the Non-Bypassability Theorem of RS-PAA, which establishes that under complete mediation and an explicit containment precondition, no agent, framework, or prompt-injection path drives an unauthorized irreversible action to completion. We operationalize recovery estimation using UndoBench's Conditional Recovery Success Rate (CRSR), which conditions recovery on nominal competence. The dissertation proposes a two-part experimental protocol but reports no experiments. The central hypothesis remains falsifiable and refutable.

Keywords: authorization boundary, pre-action authorization, non-compensability, feasibility oracle, near miss, infeasibility recognition, reward hacking, agent governance, admissible policy, threat-model-dependent enforcement.

