Chapter 1. Empirical Motivation and Conceptual Landscape

1.1 The Authorization Boundary Problem

An autonomous agent operates under a governing policy \mathcal{P} specifying which actions are permitted for task \tau. The policy is not identical to technical access controls. A resource may be technically accessible while action upon it is not authorized. This gap has three components: access authorization, action authorization, and effect verification.

1.2 Documented Behaviors

MasDrift documents unauthorized actions in 2.7–19.8% of tasks in centralized hierarchies. The failure follows from coordination rather than model strength: a heterogeneous case study confirms this, and 71.9% of losses remain at the first handoff even at three levels of hierarchy depth. FeasiGen documents false-continue rates of up to 73.9% on infeasible tasks. RS-PAA demonstrates that agent-side hooks are bypassed every time in a controlled testbed, while resource-side enforcement reduces attacker success to 0% on cross-framework bypass and multi-step aggregation, with 0% false-deny on benign traffic. UndoBench reports that nominal competence reaches 83.54% while conditional recovery success falls to 46.72%, with naive retry producing duplicate external effects in 53.33% of trials.

1.3 The Conceptual Gap

Existing frameworks measure either the agent's choices or the enforcement layer's capacity, rarely both in a controlled factorial structure. The contribution of this framework is the controlled factorial integration of feasibility class, blockade condition, policy disclosure, and safeguard configuration, with independent measurement of proposal behavior, constraint loss, near-miss events, outcome distribution, failure, refusal, abandonment, recovery, privilege escalation, and infeasibility recognition.

