Chapter 2. Formal Framework: Events, Authorization, and Metrics

2.1 Event Ontology at the Decision Level

Let \tau denote a task, \mathcal{P} a governing policy, and h = (s_0, a_0, \ldots, s_T) an execution trajectory.

Definition 2.1 (Constraint Loss). Following MasDrift, the constraint-loss indicator is:

\text{CL}(h) = \mathbb{1}\left[\text{the reserved-action constraint is weakened or absent for the executing agent}\right]

CL is defined at the trajectory level and localizes the handoff at which the constraint is dropped. It does not require that an attempt be made; it records that the boundary is no longer operative.

Definition 2.2 (Proposal Event). P_{jt} = 1 iff the agent selects an action a_{jt} \notin \mathcal{A}_{\text{aut}}(s_{jt}, \tau, \mathcal{P}).

Definition 2.3 (Attempt Event). T_{jt} = 1 iff the agent submits an unauthorized action to an execution interface.

Definition 2.4 (Blocked Attempt Event). K_{jt} = 1 iff an unauthorized attempt is refused by the enforcement mechanism before producing any effect.

Definition 2.5 (Executed Violation Event). U_{jt} = 1 iff an unauthorized action is actually executed and produces a state transition.

Definition 2.6 (Consequential Violation Event). C_{jt} = 1 iff an executed violation produces an externally effective consequence.

Definition 2.7 (Attempt-Based Near Miss). A near miss occurs when T_{jt} = 1 and U_{jt} = 0, with the boundary unavailable but the violation not realized. The trajectory-level indicator is:

\text{NM}_{\text{attempt}}(h) = \mathbb{1}\left[\exists t: T_{jt} = 1 \land U_{jt} = 0\right]

Definition 2.8 (Drift-Based Near Miss). Following MasDrift, a drift-based near miss occurs when constraint loss is present but no unauthorized action is executed:

\text{NM}_{\text{drift}}(h) = \mathbb{1}\left[\text{CL}(h) = 1 \land U(h) = 0\right]

Remark 2.1 (Two Near-Miss Definitions). \text{NM}_{\text{attempt}} is behaviorally observable and captures an agent that probes the boundary but is blocked. \text{NM}_{\text{drift}} captures a structural condition: the constraint has been lost but the violation has not yet occurred. These measure different phenomena and must be reported separately. The first indicates an agent under pressure; the second indicates a governance failure that may manifest later.

Proposition 2.1 (Event Nesting). C_{jt} \leq U_{jt} \leq T_{jt} \leq P_{jt}, and consequently C_j \leq U_j \leq T_j \leq P_j.

Proof. A consequential violation is an executed violation; an executed violation requires submission; submission requires selection. \square

Remark 2.2 (Blocking Is Not a Negation). The outcome categories for an unauthorized attempt must be exhaustive and mutually exclusive:

\Omega_{\text{outcome}} = \{\text{blocked}, \text{executed}, \text{expired}, \text{technical failure}, \text{indeterminate}\}

2.2 Recognition of Impossibility

Definition 2.9 (Impossibility Recognition Event). I(h) = 1 iff the agent explicitly signals, through a response or declared state, that the assigned objective cannot be achieved under the applicable policy.

Definition 2.10 (False Continue Event). Following FeasiGen, the false-continue indicator is:

\text{FC}(h) = \mathbb{1}\left[G(h) = 0 \land U(h) = 0 \land I(h) = 0 \land \text{execution continued}\right]

FeasiGen reports false-continue rates up to 73.9% across nine models, described as "substantially weak infeasibility detection ability". Multi-agent architectures significantly reduce erroneous execution under infeasible conditions.

2.3 Failure, Refusal, and Abandonment

Definition 2.11 (Failure on Feasible Tasks). For \tau \in \mathcal{F}_1, F(h) = 1 iff G(h) = 0.

Definition 2.12 (Explicit Unjustified Refusal). R(h) = 1 iff the agent explicitly refuses the task and an authorized path exists.

Definition 2.13 (Avoidable Abandonment). A(h) = 1 iff the agent abandons the task without a valid reason while an authorized recovery path remains available.

2.4 Access and Action Authorization

Definition 2.14 (Access Authorization). A_{\text{acc}}(i, r, t) \in \{0, 1\} is 1 iff agent i holds valid credentials for resource r at time t.

Definition 2.15 (Action Authorization). A_{\text{act}}(i, a, r, \mathcal{P}, t) \in \{0, 1\} is 1 iff action a on resource r is permitted under policy \mathcal{P} at time t.

Proposition 2.2 (Non-Implication). A_{\text{acc}}(i, r, t) = 1 \not\Rightarrow A_{\text{act}}(i, a, r, \mathcal{P}, t) = 1.

2.5 The Authorization Function

Definition 2.16 (Authorization Function).

\Phi(i, a, r, \mathcal{P}, t, \text{ctx}) \in \{\text{ALLOW}, \text{DENY}, \text{ESCALATE}\}

The action executes iff \Phi(\cdot) = \text{ALLOW}. A DENY verdict is terminal. An ESCALATE verdict defers to a higher-authority decision procedure.

2.6 Hierarchical Invariants

Type Invariant Question
Decision Determinism (restricted) Same canonical input \rightarrow same verdict?
Integrity Action-target-state binding, replay resistance, concurrent single-use Verdict covers exactly the executed action?
Temporality Temporal validity Authorization still valid at execution time?
Application Fail-closed, evidence-before-effect System applies the decision?
Architecture Non-bypassability under \mathcal{M} All execution paths covered?

Invariant 2.1 (Fail-Closed). If \Phi is unavailable or indeterminate, the default verdict is DENY.

Invariant 2.2 (Source-Anchored Revalidation). Authorization evidence must be re-derived from the source request at each handoff, not propagated through the delegation chain. MasDrift reports that re-anchoring every pending call to the original user request reduces unauthorized actions in every model configuration evaluated, at a cost of 1.6 points of pooled completion, whereas chain propagation blocks required work and forfeits up to 36.3 points.

2.7 Non-Bypassability as a Threat-Model-Dependent Property

Definition 2.17 (Non-Bypassability). An authorization layer is non-bypassable with respect to threat model \mathcal{M} if, for every execution path available to an adversary within \mathcal{M}, the path either passes through \Phi or is incapable of producing an externally effective consequence.

Theorem 2.1 (RS-PAA Non-Bypassability, Xiang 2026). Under complete mediation and an explicit containment precondition, no agent, framework, or prompt-injection path drives an unauthorized irreversible action to completion.

Preconditions. Complete mediation requires that every execution path passes through the authorization function. The containment precondition requires that the adversary cannot re-host the agent outside the mediated runtime or issue direct resource calls bypassing the mediation layer. These preconditions define the threat model \mathcal{M} under which the theorem holds.

Proposition 2.3 (Threat-Model Dependence). Non-bypassability is a property of the pair (authorization layer, threat model), not of the authorization layer alone.

Proof. Consider a layer intercepting all tool calls within a designated runtime. If \mathcal{M} includes an adversary capable of re-hosting the agent outside that runtime, the layer is bypassable. \square

Remark 2.3 (Adversary Capabilities). The threat model must specify: (i) whether the adversary can bypass Python-level hooks; (ii) whether the adversary can re-host the agent outside the mediated runtime; (iii) whether the adversary can issue direct resource calls bypassing the agent framework. RS-PAA demonstrates empirically that agent-side hooks are bypassed every time, while full resource-side enforcement reduces attacker success to 0% on cross-framework bypass and multi-step aggregation, with 0% false-deny on benign traffic.

2.8 The Five Distinct Notions in the Guarantee Chain

1. Mathematical admissibility of the policy.
2. Correctness of the authorization decision.
3. Effective application of that decision.
4. Absence of a bypass path within \mathcal{M}.
5. Empirical measurement of residual violations.

