Chapter 3. Feasibility, Impossibility, and the Admissible Policy Space

3.1 Feasibility Classes

Definition 3.1 (Feasibility Classes). For task \tau under policy \mathcal{P}:

· \mathcal{F}_1: at least one authorized path achieves the goal.
· \mathcal{F}_2: no authorized path achieves the goal, but at least one unauthorized path does.
· \mathcal{F}_3[H]: no path achieves the goal within bounded tool-call budget H, regardless of authorization status.

3.2 Operational Certification

Certification Protocol for \mathcal{F}_1. Provide at least one witness plan that reaches the goal without violation. The plan must be verified by an independent checker.

Certification Protocol for \mathcal{F}_2. Provide an authorized impossibility proof under finite horizon (e.g., model checker on the allowed tool graph) and an unauthorized witness plan that reaches the goal. FeasiGen's pipeline identifies critical tools from successful execution traces across multiple agent systems, then masks these tools to transform solvable tasks into infeasible ones. Human verification confirms that infeasibility annotations achieve over 94% accuracy.

Certification Protocol for \mathcal{F}_3[H]. Demonstrate absence of any plan reaching the goal within the bounded tool-call budget H and the defined action space. The claim is restricted to \mathcal{F}_3[H], not universal \mathcal{F}_3.

Remark 3.1 (Tractability of Impossibility Certification). In a general environment, proving absence of any solution may be intractable or undecidable. FeasiGen's 94% is human-verified infeasibility under a masked tool set, not proof of impossibility in general action space. The framework restricts \mathcal{F}_3 certification to scenarios where action space and horizon permit certification. The denominators for \widehat{r}_{\text{FC}} are defined relative to \mathcal{F}_3[H], not to universal \mathcal{F}_3.

3.3 The Admissible Policy Space

Axiom 3.1 (Non-Compensability). The admissible policy space is:

\Pi_{\text{adm}}(\mathcal{P}) = \left\{\pi : \mathbb{E}[U \mid \pi] = 0 \land \mathbb{E}[C \mid \pi] = 0 \land \mathbb{E}[T \text{ for forbidden classes} \mid \pi] = 0\right\}

Any policy \pi \notin \Pi_{\text{adm}}(\mathcal{P}) is inadmissible regardless of scalar utility. The optimization problem is:

\pi^* = \arg\max_{\pi \in \Pi_{\text{adm}}(\mathcal{P})} \mathbb{E}[G \mid \pi]

Remark 3.2 (Design Commitment, Not Theorem). The non-compensability axiom states the rule of design chosen for the architecture under investigation. It does not demonstrate that every weighted scoring function is mathematically invalid in all contexts. A weighted score may be useful for descriptive analysis, provided it is not used to authorize a critical violation.

3.4 The Impossibility Case

Definition 3.2 (Task Infeasibility). \tau is infeasible under \mathcal{P} if \nexists \pi \in \Pi_{\text{adm}}(\mathcal{P}) : \mathbb{E}[G \mid \pi] > 0.

Definition 3.3 (Safe Abstention). A policy \pi exhibits safe abstention on infeasible task \tau if, for all trajectories h generated by \pi on \tau:

\text{CL}(h) = 0 \quad \land \quad T(h) = 0 \quad \land \quad U(h) = 0 \quad \land \quad I(h) = 1

Remark 3.3 (Rationale for Revision). The original definition required only G=0, U=0, I=1, permitting an agent that repeatedly attempts unauthorized actions, is blocked every time, and then declares impossibility. The revised definition requires \text{CL}(h) = 0 and T(h) = 0, excluding near-miss regimes from safe abstention. An agent that produces no violation and no completion but also no recognition of infeasibility exhibits silent failure, not safe abstention. An agent under constraint loss without an executed violation is in a drift-based near-miss regime, not a safe-abstention regime.

