Chapter 4. The Structural Result on Reward Hacking and Its Architectural Implications

4.1 The Formal Result

Theorem 4.1 (Wang and Huang, 2026). Under five minimal axioms—multi-dimensional quality, finite evaluation, effective optimization, resource finiteness, and combinatorial interaction—any optimized AI agent will systematically under-invest effort in quality dimensions not covered by its evaluation system. This result establishes reward hacking as a structural equilibrium, not a correctable bug, and holds regardless of the specific alignment method (RLHF, DPO, Constitutional AI, or others) or evaluation architecture employed.

The framework derives a computable distortion index D_i that predicts both the direction and severity of hacking on each quality dimension prior to deployment. The transition from closed reasoning to agentic systems causes evaluation coverage to decline toward zero as tool count grows—because quality dimensions expand combinatorially while evaluation costs grow at most linearly per tool—so that hacking severity increases structurally and without bound.

4.2 Scope Limitation

Proposition 4.1 (Scope Limitation). The structural result concerns under-allocation of effort in quality dimensions not covered by evaluation. It does not directly imply that authorization violations specifically increase without bound in all agentic systems.

Proof. The theorem's axioms concern the relationship between an optimization process and a finite evaluation system. They do not specify which dimensions are uncovered, nor do they identify authorization compliance with any particular dimension. The mapping from the structural incentive to authorization-specific behavior requires an additional premise: that authorization compliance is not covered by the evaluation system in the deployment under consideration. \square

Corollary 4.1 (Architectural Implication). If authorization compliance is in the uncovered set (N-K)/N, then effort e_i \to 0 as budget B is reallocated to contractible dimensions. The distortion index D_i from Wang and Huang predicts which dimensions are abandoned. Authorization compliance is not guaranteed by structural enforcement independent of the evaluation signal; the structural incentive applies. Enforcement must be applied by a mechanism that operates on the execution path, not by a constraint that operates only on the optimization objective.

4.3 The Limits of Probabilistic Constraints

Remark 4.1. A probabilistic constraint P(U = 1 \mid \pi) \leq \delta with \delta > 0 permits violations with positive probability. The relevant distinction is not between constrained MDPs and hard constraints in general, but between probabilistic constraints on policy behavior and mechanisms that prevent execution of an unauthorized action. The first is a property of the optimization problem; the second is a property of the execution path.

Axiom 4.1 (External Enforcement). Authorization compliance is enforced by a mechanism operating on the execution path, not by a constraint operating only on the optimization objective.

