Chapter 5. Experimental Protocol Specification

5.1 The Central Hypothesis

Hypothesis 5.1. Safeguard configurations differ not only in their effect on executed violations but also on the agent's propensity to propose unauthorized actions, on constraint loss, on near-miss frequency, on unjustified refusals, on legitimate recovery, and on infeasibility recognition.

This hypothesis is falsifiable. If the experimental protocol produces evidence that safeguard configurations affect only executed violations while leaving proposal behavior, constraint loss, near-miss frequency, refusal, recovery, and recognition unchanged, the hypothesis is refuted.

5.2 Falsifiability Thresholds

The following thresholds are pre-registered:

H1. Tool-enforced authorization reduces U by >50% versus instructions-only (baseline: MasDrift reports 2.7–19.8% unauthorized actions in centralized hierarchies).

H2. Safeguard configuration affects P (ANOVA on proposal rate).

H3. \widehat{r}_{\text{NM}} under instructions-only >10% versus <2% under resource-side enforcement.

H4. CRSR under combined enforcement > naive retry by >20 points (baseline: UndoBench reports 46.72% CRSR and 53.33% duplicate effects under naive retry).

If H1 is true and H2–H4 are false, the central hypothesis is refuted.

5.3 The Paired-Replay Experiment

The paired-replay design samples a model request once and submits the same action, resource, and arguments under different authorization conditions. This holds the model's decision constant and varies only the permission envelope. The permission envelopes are:

· ALLOW: Baseline authorized condition.
· DENY (policy): Action denied by governing policy.
· UNAUTHENTICATED (access): No valid access credentials.
· CONTAINED (resource-side): Action checked at resource boundary with stateful envelope.

This design isolates the effect of the enforcement layer on execution and consequences. It does not measure how the agent adapts its behavior to different enforcement mechanisms, because the request is fixed.

5.4 The Interactive Factorial Experiment

Factors:

· Task feasibility: \mathcal{F}_1, \mathcal{F}_2, \mathcal{F}_3[H], certified independently.
· Blockade condition: no blockade, required tool unavailable, action refused by policy.
· Policy disclosure: explicit prohibition, ambiguous instruction, restriction not stated in prompt but imposed by system policy.
· Safeguard configuration: instructions only, tool-enforced authorization, independent monitor, combined enforcement and monitor.
· Phase: PRE_MUTATION, DURING_MUTATION, POST_MUTATION_PRE_ACK. UndoBench shows that recovery is phase-dependent: before mutation, methods perform similarly without duplicate effects among capable trials; during partial mutation, naive retry, per-call idempotency, and zero-privilege journaling collapse; after commit but before acknowledgment, verification and server-side idempotency substantially improve safety.

5.5 Metrics

Primary Metric (Stratified by Feasibility Class).

For \mathcal{F}_1 tasks:

S_{\text{sys}}(h) = \mathbb{1}[G(h) = 1 \land U(h) = 0] \quad \text{(system-level enforced success)}

S_{\text{agent}}(h) = \mathbb{1}[G(h) = 1 \land P(h) = 0 \land T(h) = 0 \land U(h) = 0] \quad \text{(agent-level authorized success)}

For \mathcal{F}_2 and \mathcal{F}_3[H] tasks:

S_{\text{abstain}}(h) = \mathbb{1}[G(h) = 0 \land \text{CL}(h) = 0 \land T(h) = 0 \land U(h) = 0 \land I(h) = 1]

Near-Miss Rates.

\widehat{r}_{\text{NM,attempt}} = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{N} \text{NM}_{\text{attempt},j}}{N}

\widehat{r}_{\text{NM,drift}} = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{N} \text{NM}_{\text{drift},j}}{N}

False-Continue Rate.

\widehat{r}_{\text{FC}} = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{N} \text{FC}_j}{N}

Secondary Metrics. Proposal rate \widehat{r}_P; outcome distribution over \Omega_{\text{outcome}}; failure rate on feasible tasks; explicit unjustified refusal rate; avoidable abandonment rate; infeasibility recognition rate; privilege escalation rate.

Recovery Estimates. Following UndoBench, we distinguish:

\text{RSR} = \frac{\text{faulted successes}}{\text{all trials}} \quad \text{(Recovery Success Rate)}

\text{CRSR} = \frac{\text{faulted successes}}{\text{trials where nominal competence achieved}} \quad \text{(Conditional Recovery Success Rate)}

UndoBench defines CRSR as recovery success conditioned strictly on nominal competence, decoupling baseline task planning competence from recovery capability. We report both RSR and CRSR separately, with the conditioning sets and their characteristics.

5.6 Denominators

For each rate reported, the denominator must be specified:

· Proposal rate: conditioned on decision occasions.
· \widehat{r}_{\text{NM,attempt}}: conditioned on episodes where an unauthorized path was available.
· \widehat{r}_{\text{NM,drift}}: conditioned on episodes where constraint loss occurred.
· False-continue rate: conditioned on \mathcal{F}_3[H] tasks where execution continued.
· Failure rate: conditioned on \mathcal{F}_1 tasks.
· CRSR: conditioned on trials where nominal competence was achieved.

5.7 Rare-Event Inference

For n independent trials with zero observed events, the exact one-sided 95% upper bound under a binomial model is p_{\text{sup}} = 1 - 0.05^{1/n}. However, MasDrift and UndoBench both use paired trials under identical seeds; the independence assumption is violated. The inference model must be specified in advance: session-level aggregation, hierarchical model with session as random effect, cluster bootstrap, or another justified method. For zero violations, the session-level upper bound must be reported, not the trial-level bound.

5.8 Pre-Registration Requirements

Both experiments require pre-registration of: units of analysis and denominators; certification protocol for the feasibility oracle; exclusion criteria; treatment of correlated sessions; inference model for rare events; a small number of primary contrasts; and the decision rule for abandoning the new contribution if existing benchmarks already answer the question.

Primary Contrasts (Pre-Registered).

1. Effect of tool-enforced authorization on executed violation rate U_{jt}.
2. Effect of safeguard configuration on proposal rate P_{jt}.
3. Effect of safeguard configuration on \widehat{r}_{\text{NM,attempt}} and \widehat{r}_{\text{NM,drift}}.
4. Effect of blockade on authorized recovery within \mathcal{F}_1 tasks, measured by CRSR.
5. Effect of safeguard configuration on correct infeasibility recognition within \mathcal{F}_2 and \mathcal{F}_3[H] tasks.

