Chapter 6. Novelty Conditions, Methodology, and Limitations

6.1 Positioning Against Existing Work

Construct Existing Work Contribution of This Framework
Constraint loss MasDrift (2026) Integration with attempt-based near miss
Feasibility oracle FeasiGen (2026) Certification protocol with false-continue metric
Reward hacking theorem Wang and Huang (2026) Architectural implication via distortion index
Non-bypassability RS-PAA (2026) Threat-model-dependent guarantee chain
Recovery estimation UndoBench (2026) Stratified RSR and CRSR reporting
Source-anchored revalidation MasDrift (2026) Invariant 2.2 in authorization hierarchy

Proposition 6.1 (Novelty Condition). The contribution is not the introduction of non-compensability, pre-action authorization, least-privilege evaluation, paired replay, constraint-loss detection, near-miss measurement, or impossibility recognition as isolated constructs. Each exists independently. The contribution is the controlled factorial integration of feasibility class, blockade condition, policy disclosure, and safeguard configuration, with independent measurement of proposal behavior, constraint loss, near-miss frequency, outcome distribution, failure, refusal, abandonment, recovery, privilege escalation, and infeasibility recognition.

6.2 Methodological Requirements

Requirement 6.1. The feasibility oracle must be certified operationally, not merely declared independent.

Requirement 6.2. The outcome categories for unauthorized attempts must be exhaustive and mutually exclusive.

Requirement 6.3. Recovery must be reported both as RSR and as CRSR, following UndoBench.

Requirement 6.4. The inference model for rare events must be specified in advance and compatible with observed dependence structure.

Requirement 6.5. Primary contrasts must be small in number and pre-registered.

Requirement 6.6. The distinction between proposition and intention must be maintained. An unauthorized proposal is a behavioral event, not evidence of hidden psychological intention.

Requirement 6.7. Safe abstention requires \text{CL}(h) = 0 and T(h) = 0, excluding near-miss regimes from the abstention category.

Requirement 6.8. The threat model \mathcal{M} must specify adversary capabilities: Python-level hook bypass, agent re-hosting, and direct resource calls.

6.3 Limitations

Limitation 6.1 (No Empirical Validation). The framework has not been tested experimentally. All claims are theoretical.

Limitation 6.2 (Tractability of Impossibility Certification). Proving absence of any solution may be intractable or undecidable in general environments. FeasiGen's 94% is human-verified infeasibility under a masked tool set, not proof of impossibility in general action space. The \mathcal{F}_3[H] class is restricted to bounded tool-call budgets.

Limitation 6.3 (Canonicalization Trust Assumption). The authorization function operates on a canonicalized action format with defined equivalence classes. The canonicalization step introduces a trust assumption that must be reported explicitly.

Limitation 6.4 (Threat-Model Dependence). Non-bypassability is a property of the pair (authorization layer, threat model). The guarantee does not extend beyond the specified threat model. The RS-PAA theorem holds under complete mediation and an explicit containment precondition.

Limitation 6.5 (Scope of the Structural Result). The Wang and Huang theorem concerns under-allocation in uncovered quality dimensions. It does not directly imply authorization violations increase without bound. The distortion index D_i predicts direction and severity conditional on the uncovered set, but the mapping to authorization-specific behavior requires the additional premise that authorization compliance is in the uncovered set.

Limitation 6.6 (Novelty as Hypothesis). The novelty of the factorial integration remains a hypothesis requiring systematic bibliographic comparison.

6.4 What the Framework Does Not Claim

· A general security proof or universal non-bypassability guarantee.
· An empirical demonstration that the proposed safeguards are superior.
· A definitively established bibliographic novelty.
· A proof that reward hacking necessarily produces authorization violations.
· That a weighted scoring function is mathematically invalid in all contexts.
· That determinism is a universal safety requirement.
· That the feasibility oracle has free access to perfect knowledge of impossibility.

