General Conclusion

This dissertation has developed a formal theoretical framework for evaluating authorized task completion under blockade conditions in autonomous AI agents. The framework rests on a design commitment: authorization compliance is treated as a hard admissibility requirement. Performance is optimized only among policies satisfying the specified authorization constraints. Aggregate utility scores may be reported descriptively but cannot override a failed mandatory authorization condition.

The framework makes six contributions. First, it formalizes ontologically distinct events—constraint loss, proposal, attempt, blocked attempt, executed violation, consequential violation, and two near-miss constructs—at the decision level, with trajectory-level aggregates defined explicitly. Second, it introduces an independent recognition-of-impossibility variable and distinguishes it from failure, explicit refusal, and avoidable abandonment. Third, it separates access authorization from action authorization, formalizes a pre-action authorization function, organizes invariants hierarchically, and adds source-anchored revalidation based on MasDrift's finding that re-anchoring reduces unauthorized actions at a cost of 1.6 points versus 36.3 points for chain propagation. Fourth, it grounds the structural result on reward hacking in the formal theorem of Wang and Huang, using the distortion index D_i to derive the architectural implication that enforcement must operate on the execution path. Fifth, it specifies the admissible policy space, the operational certification protocol via FeasiGen, and the five distinct notions in the guarantee chain. Sixth, it specifies stratified metrics with explicit denominators, RSR and CRSR recovery estimates following UndoBench, and rare-event inference models that account for session correlation.

The central hypothesis is that safeguard configurations differ not only in their effect on executed violations but also on proposal behavior, constraint loss, near-miss frequency, unjustified refusal, legitimate recovery, and infeasibility recognition. This hypothesis is stated as falsifiable. The framework proposes a test that could refute its own central claim.

The open problems are three. 

First, the certification of impossibility in general environments remains subject to tractability limits. 

Second, the canonicalization of arbitrary action representations remains subject to a trust assumption. 

Third, the bibliographic novelty of the factorial integration requires systematic comparison against existing benchmarks.

