Appendix A. Event Ontology: Formal Details

A.1 Decision Occasion and Trajectory Formalization

Definition A.1 (Execution Trace). An execution trace for task \tau under policy \mathcal{P} is a finite sequence

h = (s_0, a_0, r_0, o_0, s_1, a_1, r_1, o_1, \ldots, s_T)

where s_t \in \mathcal{S} is the environment state at decision occasion t, a_t \in \mathcal{A}_t is the selected action, r_t is the target resource, and o_t \in \Omega_{\text{outcome}} is the observed outcome category.

Definition A.2 (Decision Occasion Set). Let \mathcal{T}(h) = \{0, 1, \ldots, T-1\} denote the set of decision occasions in h, and D(h) = |\mathcal{T}(h)| the number of such occasions.

Definition A.3 (Authorized Action Set). For state s_t, task \tau, and policy \mathcal{P},

\mathcal{A}_{\text{aut}}(s_t, \tau, \mathcal{P}) = \left\{a \in \mathcal{A}_t : \Phi(i, a, r_t, \mathcal{P}, t, \text{ctx}) = \text{ALLOW}\right\}

A.2 Decision-Level Indicator Definitions

For each decision occasion t in episode j:

P_{jt} = \mathbb{1}\left[a_{jt} \notin \mathcal{A}_{\text{aut}}(s_{jt}, \tau_j, \mathcal{P})\right]

T_{jt} = \mathbb{1}\left[\text{an unauthorized action is submitted to an execution interface at } t\right]

K_{jt} = \mathbb{1}\left[T_{jt} = 1 \land \Phi(\cdot) = \text{DENY} \land \text{no effect produced}\right]

U_{jt} = \mathbb{1}\left[T_{jt} = 1 \land \Phi(\cdot) = \text{ALLOW} \land \text{state transition occurs}\right]

C_{jt} = \mathbb{1}\left[U_{jt} = 1 \land \text{externally effective consequence produced}\right]

A.3 Proof of Proposition 2.1 (Event Nesting)

Proposition. C_{jt} \leq U_{jt} \leq T_{jt} \leq P_{jt}, and consequently C_j \leq U_j \leq T_j \leq P_j.

Proof. We establish each inequality by contradiction.

Claim 1: C_{jt} \leq U_{jt}. Suppose C_{jt} = 1. By Definition A.2, a consequential violation requires an executed violation as a precondition. If C_{jt} = 1 and U_{jt} = 0, then an externally effective consequence was produced by an action that did not execute and produce a state transition. This contradicts the definition of consequence, which requires a state transition. Therefore C_{jt} \leq U_{jt}.

Claim 2: U_{jt} \leq T_{jt}. Suppose U_{jt} = 1 and T_{jt} = 0. Then an unauthorized action executed and produced a state transition without being submitted to an execution interface. This contradicts the definition of execution, which requires submission as the mechanism by which an action reaches the execution layer. Therefore U_{jt} \leq T_{jt}.

Claim 3: T_{jt} \leq P_{jt}. Suppose T_{jt} = 1 and P_{jt} = 0. Then an unauthorized action was submitted to an execution interface without the agent having selected an unauthorized action. This contradicts the definition of submission, which presupposes selection. Therefore T_{jt} \leq P_{jt}.

The trajectory-level inequalities follow by applying the definition of the existential aggregate:

C_j = \mathbb{1}\left[\exists t: C_{jt} = 1\right] \leq \mathbb{1}\left[\exists t: U_{jt} = 1\right] = U_j

and similarly for the remaining pairs. \square

A.4 Non-Negation of Blocking

Proposition A.1. K_{jt} is not the negation of U_{jt}.

Proof. Let \Omega_{\text{outcome}} = \{\text{blocked}, \text{executed}, \text{expired}, \text{technical failure}, \text{indeterminate}\}. By definition, these categories are mutually exclusive. If K_{jt} = 0, the attempt may belong to any of the remaining four categories, including \text{executed}. Therefore K_{jt} = 1 - U_{jt} does not hold. \square

