Appendix B. Near-Miss Constructs: Formal Derivation

B.1 Attempt-Based Near Miss

Definition B.1. The attempt-based near-miss indicator for trajectory h is:

\text{NM}_{\text{attempt}}(h) = \mathbb{1}\left[\exists t \in \mathcal{T}(h): T_{jt} = 1 \land U_{jt} = 0\right]

Proposition B.1 (Non-Implication of Safe Abstention). \text{NM}_{\text{attempt}}(h) = 1 \not\Rightarrow S_{\text{abstain}}(h) = 0 under the original definition.

Proof. Under the original Definition 3.3, S_{\text{abstain}}(h) = 1 required only G(h) = 0, U(h) = 0, I(h) = 1. A trajectory with T(h) = 1, U(h) = 0, I(h) = 1 satisfies the original definition but also satisfies \text{NM}_{\text{attempt}}(h) = 1. Therefore the original definition admitted near-miss trajectories into the safe-abstention category. \square

Corollary B.1. The revised Definition 3.3 (S_{\text{abstain}}(h) = \mathbb{1}[G(h) = 0 \land \text{CL}(h) = 0 \land T(h) = 0 \land U(h) = 0 \land I(h) = 1]) excludes all trajectories with \text{NM}_{\text{attempt}}(h) = 1.

B.2 Drift-Based Near Miss

Definition B.2. Following MasDrift, constraint loss (CL) is defined as the reserved-action constraint being "weakened or absent for the executing agent" at some handoff in the delegation chain. The drift-based near-miss indicator is:

\text{NM}_{\text{drift}}(h) = \mathbb{1}\left[\text{CL}(h) = 1 \land U(h) = 0\right]

B.3 Relationship Between Near-Miss Constructs

Proposition B.2. \text{NM}_{\text{drift}}(h) and \text{NM}_{\text{attempt}}(h) are logically independent.

Proof. We construct two trajectories:

Trajectory A: \text{CL}(h) = 1, T(h) = 0, U(h) = 0, I(h) = 1. The constraint is lost but the agent does not attempt a violation. Then \text{NM}_{\text{drift}} = 1 and \text{NM}_{\text{attempt}} = 0.

Trajectory B: \text{CL}(h) = 0, T(h) = 1, U(h) = 0, I(h) = 0. The constraint is intact but the agent attempts a violation that is blocked. Then \text{NM}_{\text{drift}} = 0 and \text{NM}_{\text{attempt}} = 1.

Since both assignments are consistent with the definitions, the indicators are logically independent. \square

