Appendix C. Feasibility Certification: Detailed Protocols

C.1 FeasiGen Pipeline for \mathcal{F}_2 Certification

FeasiGen extracts tool-calling traces from successful executions across multiple agent systems, identifies critical tools consistently shared across diverse execution strategies, and masks these tools to automatically transform solvable tasks into infeasible ones. Human verification confirms that infeasibility annotations achieve over 94% accuracy.

Algorithm C.1 (FeasiGen Certification Protocol).

Input: Task \tau, tool set \mathcal{T}_{\text{all}}, agent system \mathcal{A}.

Step 1. Execute \tau under \mathcal{A} with \mathcal{T}_{\text{all}}. Record the tool-call trace \text{tr}.

Step 2. Identify the critical tool set:

\mathcal{T}_{\text{crit}}(\tau) = \bigcap_{\text{tr} \in \mathcal{T}\mathcal{R}(\tau, \mathcal{A})} \text{tools}(\text{tr})

where \mathcal{T}\mathcal{R}(\tau, \mathcal{A}) is the set of successful traces for \tau under \mathcal{A}.

Step 3. Construct the masked environment \mathcal{T}_{\text{masked}} = \mathcal{T}_{\text{all}} \setminus \mathcal{T}_{\text{crit}}(\tau).

Step 4. Verify infeasibility under \mathcal{T}_{\text{masked}}: confirm that no authorized plan reaches the goal using only tools in \mathcal{T}_{\text{masked}}. If an authorized plan exists, the task is not \mathcal{F}_2 under the masked environment.

Step 5. Verify the unauthorized witness: confirm that at least one unauthorized plan reaches the goal under \mathcal{T}_{\text{all}}.

Output: Certified \mathcal{F}_2 task with masking set \mathcal{T}_{\text{crit}}(\tau).

Remark C.1. The 94% human-verified accuracy is relative to the masked tool set, not to universal impossibility. An alternative authorized plan using a tool outside \mathcal{T}_{\text{crit}}(\tau) would invalidate the \mathcal{F}_2 classification. Step 4 addresses this by explicit verification.

C.2 \mathcal{F}_3[H] Certification with Bounded Horizon

Definition C.1. A task \tau belongs to \mathcal{F}_3[H] if no plan (authorized or unauthorized) reaches the goal within H tool calls.

Algorithm C.2 (\mathcal{F}_3[H] Certification).

Input: Task \tau, tool set \mathcal{T}, horizon H.

Step 1. Enumerate all action sequences of length \leq H over \mathcal{T}. For finite action sets, this is finite though potentially large.

Step 2. For each sequence, check whether the goal predicate G is satisfied.

Step 3. If no sequence satisfies G, certify \tau \in \mathcal{F}_3[H].

Complexity Note. The enumeration is O(|\mathcal{T}|^H), which is polynomial in |\mathcal{T}| for fixed H but exponential in H. The framework restricts \mathcal{F}_3[H] certification to scenarios where |\mathcal{T}| and H are sufficiently small to permit complete enumeration or model-checking.

