Appendix E. Recovery Estimation: RSR and CRSR

E.1 Formal Definitions

Definition E.1 (Recovery Success Rate). Let \mathcal{F} denote the event that a fault is injected. Then:

\text{RSR} = P(\text{Success} \mid \mathcal{F})

Definition E.2 (Conditional Recovery Success Rate). Let \mathcal{C} denote the event that nominal competence is achieved in the paired control trial. Then:

\text{CRSR} = P(\text{Success} \mid \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{C})

Proposition E.1 (Law of Total Probability Decomposition). \text{RSR} can be decomposed as:

\text{RSR} = \text{CRSR} \cdot P(\mathcal{C} \mid \mathcal{F}) + P(\text{Success} \mid \mathcal{F}, \neg\mathcal{C}) \cdot P(\neg\mathcal{C} \mid \mathcal{F})

Proof. Immediate from the law of total probability conditioned on \mathcal{C} and \neg\mathcal{C}. \square

E.2 Phase-Dependent Recovery

UndoBench reports that recovery is phase-dependent:

Phase Naive Retry Per-Call Idempotency Verification / Server-Side Idempotency
PRE_MUTATION Safe Safe Safe
DURING_MUTATION Collapses Collapses Collapses
POST_MUTATION_PRE_ACK Duplicate effects Duplicate effects Substantially improves

The framework incorporates phase as a factorial factor in Chapter 5.

