Appendix F. Non-Bypassability: Theorem and Preconditions

F.1 Theorem Statement

Theorem F.1 (RS-PAA Non-Bypassability). Under complete mediation and an explicit containment precondition, no agent, framework, or prompt-injection path drives an unauthorized irreversible action to completion.

F.2 Preconditions

Definition F.1 (Complete Mediation). An authorization layer satisfies complete mediation if, for every execution path p available in the environment, p passes through the authorization function \Phi before producing any externally effective consequence.

Definition F.2 (Containment Precondition). The containment precondition is satisfied if the adversary cannot:

1. Re-host the agent outside the mediated runtime.
2. Issue direct resource calls bypassing the mediation layer.
3. Bypass the Python-level hooks that implement \Phi.

F.3 Proof Sketch

Proof Sketch. Assume complete mediation and the containment precondition. Let p be any execution path available to the adversary. By complete mediation, p passes through \Phi. By the containment precondition, the adversary cannot construct an alternative path p' that bypasses \Phi. Therefore every path either passes through \Phi or is incapable of producing an externally effective consequence. Since \Phi denies unauthorized irreversible actions by definition, no unauthorized irreversible action reaches completion. \square

F.4 Empirical Validation

RS-PAA reports:

· Agent-side hooks: bypassed every time.
· Full RS-PAA: 0% attacker success on cross-framework bypass, multi-step aggregation, and cross-session accumulation.
· Full RS-PAA: 0% false-deny on benign traffic.
· Stateless resource-side monitor: admits aggregation attacks.

F.5 Threat-Model Dependence Proof (Proposition 2.3)

Proposition. Non-bypassability is a property of the pair (authorization layer, threat model), not of the authorization layer alone.

Proof. Let \mathcal{L} be an authorization layer that intercepts all tool calls within a designated runtime \mathcal{R}. Consider two threat models:

· \mathcal{M}_1: the adversary cannot re-host the agent outside \mathcal{R} and cannot issue direct resource calls.
· \mathcal{M}_2: the adversary can re-host the agent outside \mathcal{R} and can issue direct resource calls.

Under \mathcal{M}_1, \mathcal{L} satisfies complete mediation and the containment precondition, so \mathcal{L} is non-bypassable. Under \mathcal{M}_2, the adversary can re-host the agent or issue direct calls, bypassing \mathcal{L}. Therefore \mathcal{L} is bypassable under \mathcal{M}_2. Since the same layer \mathcal{L} has different non-bypassability properties under different threat models, non-bypassability is a property of the pair. \square

