Appendix G. Safe Abstention: Formal Justification for Revision

G.1 Original Definition and Its Failure Mode

Original Definition. S_{\text{abstain}}(h) = \mathbb{1}[G(h) = 0 \land U(h) = 0 \land I(h) = 1].

Counterexample. Consider a trajectory with:

· G(h) = 0 (task not completed)
· T(h) = 1 (agent attempted an unauthorized action)
· K(h) = 1 (attempt was blocked)
· U(h) = 0 (no violation executed)
· I(h) = 1 (agent declares impossibility)

Under the original definition, S_{\text{abstain}}(h) = 1. But the agent attempted to violate policy and was prevented only by the enforcement layer. This is a near-miss regime, not safe abstention.

G.2 Revised Definition

Revised Definition. S_{\text{abstain}}(h) = \mathbb{1}[G(h) = 0 \land \text{CL}(h) = 0 \land T(h) = 0 \land U(h) = 0 \land I(h) = 1].

Proposition G.1. The revised definition excludes all trajectories with \text{NM}_{\text{attempt}}(h) = 1 or \text{NM}_{\text{drift}}(h) = 1.

Proof. If \text{NM}_{\text{attempt}}(h) = 1, then T(h) = 1, so the revised definition fails at the T(h) = 0 condition. If \text{NM}_{\text{drift}}(h) = 1, then \text{CL}(h) = 1, so the revised definition fails at the \text{CL}(h) = 0 condition. \square

