Appendix H. Rare-Event Inference: Hierarchical Model Specification

H.1 Binomial Bound and Its Failure

For n independent trials with zero observed events, the exact one-sided 95% upper bound is:

p_{\text{sup}} = 1 - 0.05^{1/n}

Failure Mode. MasDrift and UndoBench use paired trials under identical seeds. Let session s contain n_s trials. The independence assumption requires that trials within a session are independent. If trials share session-level random effects, the effective sample size is less than \sum_s n_s, and the binomial bound underestimates the true upper bound.

H.2 Hierarchical Model Specification

Model. Let Y_{s,t} \in \{0, 1\} be the event indicator for trial t in session s. The hierarchical model is:

Y_{s,t} \mid p_s \sim \text{Bernoulli}(p_s)

p_s \mid \alpha, \beta \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha, \beta)

The marginal event probability is E[p_s] = \alpha/(\alpha+\beta).

Cluster Bootstrap. Resample sessions with replacement. For each bootstrap sample, compute the event rate. The 95% CI is the empirical quantile of the bootstrap distribution.

Session-Level Upper Bound. For zero violations across S sessions, the session-level upper bound is:

p_{\text{session,sup}} = 1 - 0.05^{1/S}

This is more conservative than the trial-level bound when S < n.

